## Archive format
The MINIX archive format uses the ACK 0xFF65 magic number, seen in the ACK
sourcecode here (in octal form):
https://github.com/davidgiven/ack/blob/cd8805a0ad462c120ef394225c73ce725d271740/h/arch.h#L10

```
$ hd ./misc/libc-original.a | head -n1
00000000  65 ff 63 6c 65 61 6e 75  70 2e 73 00 00 00 00 00  |e.cleanup.s.....|
```

This is different to the archives produced by ar86 and will need to taken into
consideration if cross-compiling libc.a for MINIX.

Archive spec (taken from the MINIX 1 book):
```
MAGIC_NUMBER
(header, file)
(header, file)
(header, file)
(header, file)
...
```

where each 26 byte header consists of
```
file name                 (14 bytes)
time of last modification  (4 bytes)
user id       (truncated to 1 byte)
group id                   (1 byte)
file mode protection bits  (2 bytes)
file length                (4 bytes)
```

Each file that has an odd number of bytes is padded with an extra zero byte to
make it even. The extra zero byte does not count towards the file size field.
The two longs in the header are high-order word first to be V7 compatible.

## Library format
The libc.a archive in MINIX contains packed assembly code rather than object
files. The src/commands/libpack.c src/commands/libupack.c tools can be used to
convert between normal assembly and packed assembly formats. It's a
straightforward encoding mapping the most used assembly instructions to single
non-ascii byte values and leaving the rest of the assembly code as-is.
